    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "1. **Define the composition of functions:**\n   The function \\( f \\) is defined as \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\). By the definition of function composition, this means that applied first is \\( r \\), then \\( p \\), and finally \\( q \\). Therefore, the function \\( f \\) can be written as:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = q(p(r(x)))\n   \\]\n\n2. **To find the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\):**\n   The inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) needs to satisfy the equation:\n   \\[\n   f(f^{-1}(x)) = x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad f^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n   \\]\n   Since \\( f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\), we need \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   f(f^{-1}(x)) = q(p(r(f^{-1}(x)))) = p(r(f^{-1}(x))) = r(f^{-1}(x))\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(f(x)) = f^{-1}(q(p(r(x)))) = p(r(f^{-1}(x)))\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the composition of inverse functions:**\n   To satisfy the conditions, we need to find an expression for \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) in terms of the composition of inverses. Specifically, we need to find:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x)))\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verify the composition of inverses:**\n   Let’s verify this:\n   - Apply \\( f \\) to \\( f^{-1}(x) \\):\n     \\[\n     f(f^{-1}(x)) = f(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x))))\n     \\]\n     By the definition of \\( f \\):\n     \\[\n     f(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x)))) = q(p(r(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x))))))\n     \\]\n     Since \\( r(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x)))) = r^{-1}(x) \\):\n     \\[\n     q(p(r(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x))))) = q(p(r^{-1}(x)))\n     \\]\n     And since \\( p(r^{-1}(x)) = x \\) because of the definition of \\( r^{-1} \\):\n     \\[\n     q(p(r^{-1}(x))) = q(x) = x\n     \\]\n     Therefore:\n     \\[\n     f(f^{-1}(x)) = x\n     \\]\n\n   - Apply \\( f^{-1} \\) to \\( x \\):\n     \\[\n     f^{-1}(f(x)) = f^{-1}(q(p(r(x))))\n     \\]\n     By the definition of \\( f^{-1} \\):\n     \\[\n     f^{-1}(q(p(r(x)))) = p^{-1}(r^{-1}(q^{-1}(x)))\n     \\]\n     And because \\( r^{-1}(q^{-1}(x)) = r^{-1}(r(x)) = x \\):\n     \\[\n     p^{-1}(r^{-1}(q^{-1}(x))) = p^{-1}(x) = x\n     \\]\n     Therefore:\n     \\[\n     f^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n     \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   The correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(x)))\n   \\]\n   Reversing the order of the inverses, this matches option B:\n   \\[\n   f^{-1}(x) = p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}(x)\n   \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
